WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.320
Take a small piece of tissue from one newt embryo.

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.800
Place it in the side of another.

3
00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:13.420
The host builds a second neural tube.

4
00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:16.480
Where do the cells of that tube come from?

5
00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:21.640
The answer shows how one tissue can change the fate of its neighbours.

6
00:00:22.633 --> 00:00:26.933
Early in development, the embryo sorts itself into three layers.

7
00:00:27.413 --> 00:00:31.633
The outer layer, the ectoderm, makes skin and the nervous system.

8
00:00:32.513 --> 00:00:37.973
A strip of ectoderm along the back, the neural plate, rolls up into the neural tube.

9
00:00:38.873 --> 00:00:40.653
Ectoderm on the belly becomes skin.

10
00:00:41.453 --> 00:00:43.993
Both start as the same kind of surface tissue.

11
00:00:44.473 --> 00:00:45.473
So what is different?

12
00:00:46.193 --> 00:00:51.733
Underneath the back, cells that rolled inward over the dorsal lip form the mesoderm.

13
00:00:52.517 --> 00:00:55.857
Hilde Mangold used two newt species.

14
00:00:57.157 --> 00:01:00.477
Cells of one carry little pigment, so they look pale.

15
00:01:01.797 --> 00:01:04.017
Cells of the other carry pigment granules.

16
00:01:04.877 --> 00:01:07.617
The granules stay visible in thin sections.

17
00:01:08.877 --> 00:01:12.757
She cut a small piece of dorsal lip from a pale embryo.

18
00:01:13.757 --> 00:01:19.417
She placed it in the belly of a pigmented embryo, in tissue that would become belly skin.

19
00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:23.390
A second neural plate rose beside the host's own.

20
00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:27.590
It folded, and closed into a second neural tube.

21
00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:32.030
In thin sections, most of that tube was pigmented.

22
00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:34.110
So it was made of host cells.

23
00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:38.730
A narrow pale strip from the graft lay along its floor.

24
00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:43.070
In another embryo, the whole tube was host tissue.

25
00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:47.150
The pale graft lay beneath it, as a second notochord.

26
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:53.240
Host ectoderm that would have made skin had made nervous system instead.

27
00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:55.260
This is induction.

28
00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:01.380
Induction is a change in the fate of one tissue, caused by a signal from another.

29
00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:05.020
The responding tissue must be able to answer.

30
00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:07.620
That ability is called competence.

31
00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:15.000
It does not last. In amphibians, ectoderm from older embryos responds less and less.

32
00:02:15.883 --> 00:02:19.363
The dorsal lip is called the organizer.

33
00:02:19.983 --> 00:02:22.803
Its signals do more than switch on one fate.

34
00:02:23.543 --> 00:02:25.643
Its own cells become notochord.

35
00:02:26.203 --> 00:02:29.323
It recruits neighbouring mesoderm into dorsal fates.

36
00:02:29.763 --> 00:02:31.723
And it induces the ectoderm.

37
00:02:32.643 --> 00:02:36.603
The graft left one question open: what is the signal?

38
00:02:37.543 --> 00:02:42.603
Keep the rule: a cell's fate depends, in part, on the signals it receives.
